# VIGIL off-HOST backup push — environment file (copy to ~/.config/vigil/backup-push.env, chmod 600)
# =====================================================================
# Read by vigil-backup-push.service. Two required settings (+ one optional):
#
#   VIGIL_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE — the ONLY key to the encrypted backups. Read from the
#       ENVIRONMENT by both legs, NEVER passed on the command line, NEVER stored
#       inside the backup — lose it and the backups are UNRECOVERABLE by design.
#       The push transports CIPHERTEXT ONLY; this passphrase never leaves the host.
#
#   VIGIL_BASE_DIR — OPTIONAL. The offense engine home to back up = the cross-plane
#       money seam base. The unit ships VIGIL_BASE_DIR=%h/.sigil/credit-lease, the
#       SAME value every unit on the launch path sets; override it ONLY with an
#       ABSOLUTE path (no %h here) and only together with all of them —
#       `vigil doctor` refuses when they disagree.
#
#   VIGIL_PUSH_DEST — where to replicate the ENCRYPTED files. Three real transports ship.
#       LOCAL-DIRECTORY (a path, or local:<path>) — the copy lands on the local FS:
#         * a mounted remote filesystem   e.g. /mnt/backup-nfs/vigil
#         * an sshfs mount                e.g. /home/you/mnt/remote-vigil
#         * a removable disk              e.g. /media/you/backup-usb/vigil
#       RSYNC (the real rsync binary, over ssh or an rsync daemon):
#         * over ssh     rsync:user@backup-host:/srv/vigil-backups
#         * rsync daemon rsync://backup-host/vigil/backups
#       S3 (the aws CLI — the cloud/EC2 hosting profile; see docs/runbooks/AWS-DEPLOY.md):
#         * s3://your-vigil-backup-bucket/vigil
#         The host's `aws` CLI must be configured (an EC2 instance role is cleanest) with
#         s3:PutObject + s3:GetObject on the bucket/prefix. Optional second-layer encryption
#         at rest (the files are ALREADY passphrase-encrypted): set VIGIL_BACKUP_S3_SSE_KMS_KEY_ID
#         (the backup bucket's KMS CMK) and it is uploaded SSE-KMS; or VIGIL_BACKUP_S3_SSE=AES256.
#       After copying, the push VERIFIES the copy AT THE DESTINATION (re-reads the landed
#       bytes — over rsync+ssh the REMOTE hashes its own copy with sha256sum; over s3 the
#       object is downloaded back and hashed) and FAILS the run on a truncated/corrupted/
#       tampered copy — a bad off-host copy is never accepted.
#       RESIDUAL (operator-supplied): an rsync-over-ssh target needs the operator's endpoint
#       + an ssh key (or an rsyncd secret) and a Linux remote that provides `sha256sum`; an
#       s3:// target needs a configured `aws` CLI + bucket. scp:// is recognised but NOT built
#       yet — it errors clearly with the contract to implement (tools/backup/transport.py).
#
#   VIGIL_BACKUP_S3_SSE_KMS_KEY_ID — (s3:// only, optional) the KMS CMK that server-side-encrypts
#       the uploaded objects (SSE-KMS). The key id is PUBLIC (an arn/alias) — never a secret.
#   VIGIL_BACKUP_S3_SSE — (s3:// only, optional) the SSE mode when no KMS key is set (e.g. AES256).
#   VIGIL_AWS_REGION — (s3:// only) the bucket's region (falls back to AWS_REGION / AWS_DEFAULT_REGION).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# HONEST WARNING — this unit needs the NETWORK and an at-rest passphrase.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# vigil-backup-push.service runs WITH the network (PrivateNetwork=no) so it can
# reach the remote; the routine vigil-backup.service stays air-gapped. A passphrase
# in a 0600 env file is weaker than typing it interactively — see the notes in
# vigil-backup.env.example (systemd-credentials is the stronger alternative). The
# transported bytes are already encrypted, but WHO CAN READ the remote directory is
# the operator's responsibility — lock it down, and test a restore FROM the pushed copy.

VIGIL_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=change-me-to-a-long-random-passphrase
# VIGIL_BASE_DIR=/home/USER/.sigil/credit-lease
VIGIL_PUSH_DEST=/mnt/backup-remote/vigil
